{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EzDFsWYMabXhSjCDEEupAH","type":"tool_result","content":"1687\t=== APPEND C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/objective-coverage.md ===\n1688\t\n1689\t---\n1690\t\n1691\t## Chapter 5 update (2026-08-24)\n1692\t\n1693\t| Objective | First covered in | Depth | Last reviewed |\n1694\t|---|---|---|---|\n1695\t| **D1.1** | Chapter 3 *(cluster layer)* | deep | 2026-08-24 |\n1696\t| **D1.1** | Chapter 4 *(object layer)* | deep | 2026-08-24 |\n1697\t| **D1.1** | **Chapter 5** *(workload layer)* | **deep** | **2026-08-24** |\n1698\t\n1699\t**Registry row change:** `D1.1 | Kubernetes Core Concepts | Ch 3, 4, 5, 6` →\n1700\tstatus becomes **\"in progress — Ch 3, 4, and 5 covered 2026-08-24\"**.\n1701\t\n1702\t### Chapter 5 — D1.1 coverage detail\n1703\t\n1704\t`kb_tags.objectives: [\"D1.1\"]`; all nine sections carry `objectives: [\"D1.1\"]`.\n1705\t\n1706\t| Sub-topic | Depth |\n1707\t|---|---|\n1708\t| Pod as the unit of scheduling; the definition itself | **deep — closes a four-chapter gap** |\n1709\t| Shared network namespace, Pod IP, `localhost`, shared volumes | **deep — Ch 9's premise originates here** |\n1710\t| Multi-container Pods; the two-mechanism test; sidecar | moderate — ⚑ mechanism unsourced |\n1711\t| Init containers: ordering, run-to-completion, failure | moderate — ⚑ **entirely unsourced** |\n1712\t| Pod lifetime; scheduled once; replaced with a new UID | deep — **discharges Ch 4 commitment #3** |\n1713\t| Pod phase vs container state | **deep — the centerpiece; Ch 13's method depends on it** |\n1714\t| `restartPolicy` scope, values, exponential backoff | deep |\n1715\t| ServiceAccount as Pod identity | moderate — **closes Ch 4's `Ch 5 (planted)` half** |\n1716\t| Probes: three types, four mechanisms, three failure behaviors | **deep — pays Ch 3's \"and healthy\" debt** |\n1717\t| Requests vs limits; CPU throttle vs reactive OOM; units | **deep — four later chapters retrieve it** |\n1718\t| **QoS classes** | ⚑ **ABSENT — outline-mandated, cut for lack of a snapshot** |\n1719\t| **Graceful termination** | ⚑ **ABSENT — `kb_tags` claims `pod-termination`** |\n1720\t| `pod-template` | deferred to Ch 6 — correct; the tag is aspirational |\n1721\t| `kubectl` command surface | deliberately deferred to Ch 13 / Ch 16 |\n1722\t\n1723\t---\n1724\t\n1725\t## ⚑ Book-level: the authored allocations are approaching the domain ceiling\n1726\t\n1727\tKubernetes Fundamentals is **44%** of the published exam. Four chapters sit inside it and each\n1728\tpublishes an authored allocation in a differently-worded metadata line:\n1729\t\n1730\t| Chapter | Claimed | Metadata form |\n1731\t|---|---|---|\n1732\t| Ch 2 | ~9% | **the only one that discloses \"authored allocation\" inline and source-tags it** |\n1733\t| Ch 3 | ~6% | \"authored estimate\" |\n1734\t| Ch 4 | ~6% | no caveat at all |\n1735\t| **Ch 5** | **7%** | \"This book's allocation\" — no caveat, no tag |\n1736\t| **Subtotal** | **28% of 44%** | |\n1737\t\n1738\tThat leaves **16 points for Chapter 6 alone** — larger than Chapters 3, 4, and 5 combined.\n1739\tEither the allocations need rebalancing or Chapter 6's will have to be authored to fit a\n1740\tresidue rather than derived from its content. **Settle before Chapter 6 drafts.**\n1741\t\n1742\tThis is also the concrete reason to act on Stage 13's T4 rather than defer it: four chapters\n1743\tpublish an authored number and **one** of them tells the reader it is authored. Chapter 1\n1744\tcommitted the book to that disclosure in prose. **Conform Ch 5 to Chapter 2's shape; flag\n1745\tCh 3 and Ch 4 for `reconcile.py`.** Note the competency separator also drifts three ways\n1746\t(`— competency: X` / `— X` / `(X)`); pick one.\n1747\t\n1748\t---\n1749\t\n1750\t## ⚑ Ethical-guardrail status — Chapter 5\n1751\t\n1752\t| Chapter | Guardrail #8 | Note |\n1753\t|---|---|---|\n1754\t| Ch 1 | pass | |\n1755\t| Ch 2 | pass | models the compliant phrasing |\n1756\t| Ch 3 | **FAIL — open** | six unverifiable exam-frequency assertions |\n1757\t| Ch 4 | BORDERLINE | five practitioner-prevalence superlatives |\n1758\t| **Ch 5** | **BORDERLINE** | **four exam-frequency assertions + prevalence superlatives** |\n1759\t\n1760\t**This diverges from Stage 13, which recorded a pass.** Stage 13's reasoning — that the\n1761\tsubtitle's \"worth points\" is the only soft claim and is defended structurally in §9 — is\n1762\tcorrect about the subtitle. It is not correct that the chapter carries no unhedged frequency\n1763\tclaims. Four assertions about **exam behavior** are present in the finalized text:\n1764\t\n1765\t1. §3 — \"**This is the part the exam cares about.**\"\n1766\t2. §5 — \"**both of those breadths are tested**\"\n1767\t3. §7 — \"the consequence of failure. **That's what gets tested**\"\n1768\t4. Bearings #1 A1 — \"**It will cost you points here**\"\n1769\t\n1770\tPlus the practitioner-prevalence register Chapter 4 was flagged for: \"Readers consistently\n1771\tassume,\" \"the probe people most often reverse,\" \"Practitioners find this genuinely surprising\n1772\tthe first time,\" \"the part most people don't know,\" \"the easiest carry-over error to make.\"\n1773\t\n1774\tThese are mild and the underlying judgments are very likely right. But they are unsourced\n1775\tclaims about what an exam does — **Chapter 3's specific flagged failure mode, which Chapter 4\n1776\thad cleanly avoided.** A second chapter drifting back into the construction while Chapter 3's\n1777\tremediation is still open is the signal worth having. **Hedging all four costs about six\n1778\twords.** Author call; not marked failing.\n1779\t\n1780\t**Everything else on the Part 14 checklist passes, several unusually well:**\n1781\t\n1782\t- **No statistics of any kind.** No invented pass rates, no \"73% of breaches.\" Every number is\n1783\t  a sourced mechanism value (300 s, 10s/20s/40s, 5-minute cap, 10-minute reset, HTTP\n1784\t  ≥200/<400, v1.22, the `1m` floor).\n1785\t- **Subject dignity (v5.7): clean.** Every wry beat lands on the practitioner. None lands on\n1786\t  anyone harmed by a failure.\n1787\t- **Simplification acknowledged.** Two Dead Reckoning blocks; §7 explicitly declines to teach\n1788\t  probe tuning and says why; §8 names the minor resource types and stops; §5 states outright\n1789\t  that \"the phase cannot tell you that.\"\n1790\t- **No strawmanning.** §2's rebuttal of \"one process per Pod\" engages the doctrine on the\n1791\t  merits and declines the opposite absolutism.\n1792\t\n1793\t**⚑ The condition on the \"authority claims\" pass.** Five load-bearing claim clusters are stated\n1794\tconfidently in prose while carrying no source tag, flagged only in HTML comments the reader\n1795\tnever sees: init-container semantics (§3), the \"two main ways\" framing (§2), \"smallest\n1796\tdeployable unit\" (§9), the PodSpec identity (§1), and probe-type/mechanism orthogonality (§7).\n1797\tAgainst guardrail #4 — *never claim certainty where legitimate uncertainty exists* — **an\n1798\tinvisible comment is not a hedge to the reader.** The draft handled this correctly by not\n1799\tfabricating tags. **This item passes on the condition that the five snapshots are materialized\n1800\tand the claims tagged before publication.** If any cannot be, the affected prose must be\n1801\tsoftened to an explicit authorial gloss, and the two graded items whose correct answers depend\n1802\ton untagged claims — **Practice Q17 and Practice Q10's distractor-C rebuttal** — must be\n1803\treworked or cut.\n1804\t\n1805\t---\n1806\t\n1807\t## ✅ Gate status — the one that improved\n1808\t\n1809\t**Chapter 5 has a real fact-accuracy audit, and its blocking findings were remediated.**\n1810\tStage 6 inspected 148 claims (81 tagged-and-verified, 17 untagged FAIL, 3 contradicted FAIL,\n1811\t17 WARN) and all three contradicted claims plus one over-tagging WARN were fixed in the\n1812\tfinalized chapter. **This is the first chapter in the book where a blocking fact-accuracy\n1813\tfinding was raised and discharged.**\n1814\t\n1815\tStructural: **0 fail / 0 warn / 30 pass.**\n1816\t\n1817\t⚑ **Chapter 4 still has no audit at all**, and Chapter 5's Stage 6 identifies the shared root\n1818\tcause: Stage 6's declared input is `draft-v2.md`, which does not exist at that point in the\n1819\tgraph. **Correct the Stage 6 input resolution to `draft-v1.md`** and both chapters are fixed\n1820\tat the source.\n1821\t\n1822\t=== END APPEND ===\n1823\t\n1824\t=== APPEND C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/retrieval-log.md ===\n1825\t\n1826\t---\n1827\t\n1828\t## Chapter 5 update (2026-08-24)\n1829\t\n1830\t**8 tagged in-budget items · graded pool 38 (15 Bearings + 23 Practice) · rate = 21.1%.**\n1831\tB3's rung for Chapter 5 is **20%. Cleared.** Two further tagged items sit in Soundings,\n1832\texcluded from the budget by B3.\n1833\t\n1834\t**Chapter 5 is the first chapter to draw from three predecessors.** Ch 4 drew from two, Ch 3\n1835\tfrom one.\n1836\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"839b721c-4161-4462-926d-1dd8a865c1c5","uuid":"1b2b6b46-597d-4ae3-8dfe-9ff775126d00","timestamp":"2026-08-24T13:07:19.817Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-05\\kb-manifest.md","content":"=== APPEND C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/objective-coverage.md ===\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 5 update (2026-08-24)\n\n| Objective | First covered in | Depth | Last reviewed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **D1.1** | Chapter 3 *(cluster layer)* | deep | 2026-08-24 |\n| **D1.1** | Chapter 4 *(object layer)* | deep | 2026-08-24 |\n| **D1.1** | **Chapter 5** *(workload layer)* | **deep** | **2026-08-24** |\n\n**Registry row change:** `D1.1 | Kubernetes Core Concepts | Ch 3, 4, 5, 6` →\nstatus becomes **\"in progress — Ch 3, 4, and 5 covered 2026-08-24\"**.\n\n### Chapter 5 — D1.1 coverage detail\n\n`kb_tags.objectives: [\"D1.1\"]`; all nine sections carry `objectives: [\"D1.1\"]`.\n\n| Sub-topic | Depth |\n|---|---|\n| Pod as the unit of scheduling; the definition itself | **deep — closes a four-chapter gap** |\n| Shared network namespace, Pod IP, `localhost`, shared volumes | **deep — Ch 9's premise originates here** |\n| Multi-container Pods; the two-mechanism test; sidecar | moderate — ⚑ mechanism unsourced |\n| Init containers: ordering, run-to-completion, failure | moderate — ⚑ **entirely unsourced** |\n| Pod lifetime; scheduled once; replaced with a new UID | deep — **discharges Ch 4 commitment #3** |\n| Pod phase vs container state | **deep — the centerpiece; Ch 13's method depends on it** |\n| `restartPolicy` scope, values, exponential backoff | deep |\n| ServiceAccount as Pod identity | moderate — **closes Ch 4's `Ch 5 (planted)` half** |\n| Probes: three types, four mechanisms, three failure behaviors | **deep — pays Ch 3's \"and healthy\" debt** |\n| Requests vs limits; CPU throttle vs reactive OOM; units | **deep — four later chapters retrieve it** |\n| **QoS classes** | ⚑ **ABSENT — outline-mandated, cut for lack of a snapshot** |\n| **Graceful termination** | ⚑ **ABSENT — `kb_tags` claims `pod-termination`** |\n| `pod-template` | deferred to Ch 6 — correct; the tag is aspirational |\n| `kubectl` command surface | deliberately deferred to Ch 13 / Ch 16 |\n\n---\n\n## ⚑ Book-level: the authored allocations are approaching the domain ceiling\n\nKubernetes Fundamentals is **44%** of the published exam. Four chapters sit inside it and each\npublishes an authored allocation in a differently-worded metadata line:\n\n| Chapter | Claimed | Metadata form |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ch 2 | ~9% | **the only one that discloses \"authored allocation\" inline and source-tags it** |\n| Ch 3 | ~6% | \"authored estimate\" |\n| Ch 4 | ~6% | no caveat at all |\n| **Ch 5** | **7%** | \"This book's allocation\" — no caveat, no tag |\n| **Subtotal** | **28% of 44%** | |\n\nThat leaves **16 points for Chapter 6 alone** — larger than Chapters 3, 4, and 5 combined.\nEither the allocations need rebalancing or Chapter 6's will have to be authored to fit a\nresidue rather than derived from its content. **Settle before Chapter 6 drafts.**\n\nThis is also the concrete reason to act on Stage 13's T4 rather than defer it: four chapters\npublish an authored number and **one** of them tells the reader it is authored. Chapter 1\ncommitted the book to that disclosure in prose. **Conform Ch 5 to Chapter 2's shape; flag\nCh 3 and Ch 4 for `reconcile.py`.** Note the competency separator also drifts three ways\n(`— competency: X` / `— X` / `(X)`); pick one.\n\n---\n\n## ⚑ Ethical-guardrail status — Chapter 5\n\n| Chapter | Guardrail #8 | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ch 1 | pass | |\n| Ch 2 | pass | models the compliant phrasing |\n| Ch 3 | **FAIL — open** | six unverifiable exam-frequency assertions |\n| Ch 4 | BORDERLINE | five practitioner-prevalence superlatives |\n| **Ch 5** | **BORDERLINE** | **four exam-frequency assertions + prevalence superlatives** |\n\n**This diverges from Stage 13, which recorded a pass.** Stage 13's reasoning — that the\nsubtitle's \"worth points\" is the only soft claim and is defended structurally in §9 — is\ncorrect about the subtitle. It is not correct that the chapter carries no unhedged frequency\nclaims. Four assertions about **exam behavior** are present in the finalized text:\n\n1. §3 — \"**This is the part the exam cares about.**\"\n2. §5 — \"**both of those breadths are tested**\"\n3. §7 — \"the consequence of failure. **That's what gets tested**\"\n4. Bearings #1 A1 — \"**It will cost you points here**\"\n\nPlus the practitioner-prevalence register Chapter 4 was flagged for: \"Readers consistently\nassume,\" \"the probe people most often reverse,\" \"Practitioners find this genuinely surprising\nthe first time,\" \"the part most people don't know,\" \"the easiest carry-over error to make.\"\n\nThese are mild and the underlying judgments are very likely right. But they are unsourced\nclaims about what an exam does — **Chapter 3's specific flagged failure mode, which Chapter 4\nhad cleanly avoided.** A second chapter drifting back into the construction while Chapter 3's\nremediation is still open is the signal worth having. **Hedging all four costs about six\nwords.** Author call; not marked failing.\n\n**Everything else on the Part 14 checklist passes, several unusually well:**\n\n- **No statistics of any kind.** No invented pass rates, no \"73% of breaches.\" Every number is\n  a sourced mechanism value (300 s, 10s/20s/40s, 5-minute cap, 10-minute reset, HTTP\n  ≥200/<400, v1.22, the `1m` floor).\n- **Subject dignity (v5.7): clean.** Every wry beat lands on the practitioner. None lands on\n  anyone harmed by a failure.\n- **Simplification acknowledged.** Two Dead Reckoning blocks; §7 explicitly declines to teach\n  probe tuning and says why; §8 names the minor resource types and stops; §5 states outright\n  that \"the phase cannot tell you that.\"\n- **No strawmanning.** §2's rebuttal of \"one process per Pod\" engages the doctrine on the\n  merits and declines the opposite absolutism.\n\n**⚑ The condition on the \"authority claims\" pass.** Five load-bearing claim clusters are stated\nconfidently in prose while carrying no source tag, flagged only in HTML comments the reader\nnever sees: init-container semantics (§3), the \"two main ways\" framing (§2), \"smallest\ndeployable unit\" (§9), the PodSpec identity (§1), and probe-type/mechanism orthogonality (§7).\nAgainst guardrail #4 — *never claim certainty where legitimate uncertainty exists* — **an\ninvisible comment is not a hedge to the reader.** The draft handled this correctly by not\nfabricating tags. **This item passes on the condition that the five snapshots are materialized\nand the claims tagged before publication.** If any cannot be, the affected prose must be\nsoftened to an explicit authorial gloss, and the two graded items whose correct answers depend\non untagged claims — **Practice Q17 and Practice Q10's distractor-C rebuttal** — must be\nreworked or cut.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Gate status — the one that improved\n\n**Chapter 5 has a real fact-accuracy audit, and its blocking findings were remediated.**\nStage 6 inspected 148 claims (81 tagged-and-verified, 17 untagged FAIL, 3 contradicted FAIL,\n17 WARN) and all three contradicted claims plus one over-tagging WARN were fixed in the\nfinalized chapter. **This is the first chapter in the book where a blocking fact-accuracy\nfinding was raised and discharged.**\n\nStructural: **0 fail / 0 warn / 30 pass.**\n\n⚑ **Chapter 4 still has no audit at all**, and Chapter 5's Stage 6 identifies the shared root\ncause: Stage 6's declared input is `draft-v2.md`, which does not exist at that point in the\ngraph. **Correct the Stage 6 input resolution to `draft-v1.md`** and both chapters are fixed\nat the source.\n\n=== END APPEND ===\n\n=== APPEND C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp\\..\\Book-KCNA/knowledge-base/retrieval-log.md ===\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 5 update (2026-08-24)\n\n**8 tagged in-budget items · graded pool 38 (15 Bearings + 23 Practice) · rate = 21.1%.**\nB3's rung for Chapter 5 is **20%. Cleared.** Two further tagged items sit in Soundings,\nexcluded from the budget by B3.\n\n**Chapter 5 is the first chapter to draw from three predecessors.** Ch 4 drew from two, Ch 3\nfrom one.\n","numLines":150,"startLine":1687,"totalLines":1972}}}
